# Instructions

You will build 3 packages:
 - one for the operator
 - one for the shim (to run in each dockerfile)
 - one for the shimshimney cli tool

# Operator

Build a k8s operator with operator-sdk.

When the scaffolding has been created, make a git commit to keep the rest of the code separate from the scaffolding.

Specification:

1. The operator or controller should have 2 http endpoint that can be hit from outside the cluster.
- /rebuild  -> hits the endpoint of every pod that has been registered.
- /register -> each pod that runs the shim should hit this endpoint to register itself with the operator.
- /heartbeat -> each pod that runs the shim should hit this endpoint to let the operator know that it is still alive.
- /health -> the operator should return 200 if it is healthy and running.

2. Can the operator work out which pod the /register command came from? Or should the `from` info be sent along with?
I think we can avoid labels if possible

3. The port that the pod listens on should be sent to the operator as part of the register payload.

4. The operator should create a k8s service to each pod that runs the shim, with target port being the port that was given.

5. When /rebuild is hit, the operator should hit the endpoint of every pod that has been registered. This should rebuild
the code in the pod, thereby allowing the code to update without having to rebuild the container which is time consuming.

# The shim

The shim needs to update its heartbeat on a regular basis to the operator.
Shim should have endpoints:
- /heartbeat -> hits the operator to let it know that it is still alive.
- /health -> returns 200 if the shim is healthy and running.
- /rebuild -> hits the operator to trigger a rebuild of the code in the pod.

# The example

/example
  /projects
     /dockerfile
     /api
     /common
     /backend-1
     /backend-2
     ...


Each project should be a golang project which is built by a script then deployed with k8s.

It should be deployed to a k3s cluster, the setup for that should automated.

- Each individual project should build in parallel and be deployed to the cluster.

- The operator should be able to rebuild the code in each pod without having to rebuild the container. This means the k8s
must handle some sort of volume mount or shared volume that allows the code to be updated in the pod without rebuilding the container.

- The shim should be the entrypoint / cmd for the dockerfile

# common package

this should contain a logger which is used in all the backends.

there should be 8 backends, each should just respond with a simple "hello world" message and the name of the backend.

# The shimshimney cli tool

- Use cobra to make a shimshimney cli program that can be used to interact with the operator and the shim.
- The cli should have commands to:
  - list all registered pods and their status
  - trigger a rebuild of all registered pods
  - check the health of the operator and shim

(then in the operator api add all this functionaility if not previously mentioned)

# The config file

The config file should be a yaml file that contains the following information:
- The external url of the operator api
- The "build" command that should be run in the pod to rebuild the code for a static container
- The "run" command that should be run in the pod to run the code for a static container

Build mode is "SHIMNEY_MODE=hot" and "SHIMNEY_MODE=cold" for hot and cold builds respectively. The operator should be able to handle both modes.

# The dockerfile

The dockerfiles should run from the root of the project so that the code can be built together to make use of go mod and go workspaces
to allow for shared code between the projects, like the "common" package.